                <pb facs="0446" n="366" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg366"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            Brun</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-10-25"><hi rendition="#italic">October 25,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 351.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor, in reply, to your note of
                    the 19th instant, to inform you that I have to-day instructed the minister of
                    the United States to Salvador to afford his protection to Danish subjects and
                    interests in the Republic named.</p>
                <p>Mr. Merry’s protection will consist of the exercise of his good offices with the
                    assent of the Government of Salvador, and will, of course, involve no assumption
                    of representative functions on behalf of the Government of Denmark.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
